ARCH_SECTIONS = """
<h2>Trust boundaries</h2>
<p>The design assumes the searching agent will, if permitted, optimise the scorer instead of the
score. Four boundaries make that unprofitable:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The scorer cannot be edited.</strong> <code>rk-harness</code> is mounted read-only
into the container, and the container refuses to start unless the sha256 over the ten verifier
files (coeffrep, orderconditions, verifier, costmodel, evaluator, problems, and the four fixture
files) matches a pinned value. Every archive record carries the verifier hash that scored it.</li>
<li><strong>No credential reaches the agent.</strong> The GitHub token stays on the host: the
container receives a filtered environment, only ever <em>commits</em> into the mounted
checkouts, and the host watchdog pushes with the owner's own credentials.</li>
<li><strong>The model never grades itself.</strong> Confidence tiers
(heldout_verified / search_only / unreplicated) and hypothesis verdicts
(supported / refuted / inconclusive) are computed by code from the archive. The tier strings do
not appear in any LLM prompt template; a directive carrying an unknown key &mdash; including a
tier &mdash; is rejected, not ignored.</li>
<li><strong>Model-written code is quarantined.</strong> A model-authored problem executes only
after an AST-checked import allowlist (math only, no dunders, no I/O), determinism and time
bounds, a range check, a reference solution, and a promotion gate; it can then join only the
held-out set, so the optimiser can never be steered toward a problem the model invented.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The cycle loop</h2>
<p>The runner executes one idempotent cycle at a time; a crash at any point costs at most one
cycle because the archive is append-only with fsync, state is written atomically, and replay
discards a torn trailing line. Each cycle: replay the archive &rarr; ask the encourager for an
action &rarr; produce candidates &rarr; verify each &rarr; evaluate in Q15 under three cost
models at equal cycle budget &rarr; assign a tier against the cell's incumbent &rarr; append
&rarr; resolve open hypotheses &rarr; regenerate the findings site &rarr; commit &rarr; save
state.</p>

<h2>Verification, in order</h2>
<p>Nine checks, cheap before expensive, all pure code: NOT_EXPLICIT, ROW_SUM_INCONSISTENT,
DYADIC_IMPOSSIBLE (order &ge; 3 with all-dyadic coefficients is provably empty &mdash; the
order-3 condition sums to 1/3, which no dyadic rational equals), ORDER_NOT_MET (exact rational
residuals over rooted trees, never floats), COEFF_UNREPRESENTABLE (a range check &mdash;
inexactness like 1/3 is <em>recorded</em>, never rejected), Q15_OVERFLOW (2&times; amplitude
margin), UNSTABLE, NO_ASYMPTOTIC_WINDOW (the measured-order fit found no consistent slope run),
NAN_OR_INF. Only the primary cost model's columns gate verification; the slow and AVR columns
are archive breadth.</p>

<h2>Arithmetic, exactly</h2>
<p>States are Q15 (int16, scale 2<sup>-15</sup>). A multiply is <code>(a*b) &gt;&gt; 15</code>
with an arithmetic shift &mdash; flooring toward negative infinity, matching ASRS and
deliberately not matching C's truncation. Addition overflow raises rather than wraps, so
overflow becomes a verifier rejection instead of a silent wrong answer. Coefficients are not Q15:
each is an integer-and-shift pair m/2<sup>s</sup> (rk4's 1 and kutta3's 2 do not fit [-1, 1)),
and inexact representations contribute a recorded quantisation error. Cost is driven by CSD
(non-adjacent-form) weight &mdash; 3/8 is dyadic yet costs two shifts and an add &mdash; with
every coefficient priced at min(shift-add expansion, multiply).</p>

<h2>Where candidates come from</h2>
<p>Phase 0 and 1 are exhaustive enumerations (16 points at order 2; 5,094 exact 3-stage order-3
points), so their results are proofs of optimality within the enumerated space, and the site
labels them that way. Phases 2 and 3 run CMA-ES islands over the strictly-lower triangle of A
snapped to dyadics, with b then solved <em>exactly</em> over the rationals from the b-linear
order conditions &mdash; snapping b would make order &ge; 3 impossible by the dyadic theorem.
When the requested order is not exactly solvable for a snapped A (order 4 rarely is), the
projection falls back to the highest solvable order &ge; 2 and the candidate is verified at the
order it actually achieves.</p>

<h2>The outer loop: a research partner, not a sampler</h2>
<p>The LLM (Codex, plan-billed, authenticated on the host and mounted read-only) never touches
the inner loop. It emits JSON directives that can only narrow the search &mdash; target order,
stage counts, structural constraints &mdash; validated against a schema where unknown keys are
rejections. Directives are requested every few cycles (and on every escalation) and reused in
between; calls stop at a plan-usage cap. On HYPOTHESIZE escalations it proposes falsifiable
hypotheses whose ids and verdicts are assigned by code and whose predicates must parse under a
closed grammar (a hand-written parser; no eval anywhere near model output). A literature loop
web-searches a rotating topic every ~50 cycles and feeds the digest into every prompt, so the
directives and hypotheses are grounded in existing research. The digests and the model's prose
interpretation are published on the findings site, clearly labelled and softened so the site's
banned-words guard holds.</p>

<h2>The host layer</h2>
<p>A PowerShell watchdog polls every 10 s: stale heartbeat &rarr; kill; spend over cap or disk
under 5 GB &rarr; stop; laptop on battery or foreground CPU load &rarr; pause (and resume);
plus the host-side pushes. Operational knobs &mdash; container resources, auto-stop after N
minutes/cycles, LLM throttles, watchdog thresholds &mdash; live in a workspace
<code>config.json</code> edited by <code>configure.py</code>; scientific thresholds are
deliberately not configurable because changing them invalidates the archive. A read-only watcher
window renders the whole state live; Ctrl+C in it cannot touch the run.</p>

<h2>What the pre-flight actually caught</h2>
<p>The checklist was executed as a program, not a document, and it earned its keep: the crash
-recovery drill exposed that a runner killed mid-seeding never finished writing the classical
baselines (fixed: seeding is idempotent per hash, every cycle); the trust-boundary probe showed
the original PAT could push the verifier repo despite the handoff's 403 test passing (fixed by
moving the credential out of the container entirely); the first live archive build tripped the
banned-words guard on a hypothesis containing "beats" &mdash; the guard doing
exactly its job against text nobody reviewed.</p>
"""

METHODOLOGY = """
<p>This page is the human account of how the harness works and how it was validated. Its
machine-generated counterpart is the findings site's methodology page — rebuilt by the harness
every cycle, always current on constants and thresholds — available in the
<a href="findings/methodology.html">snapshot here</a> and
<a href="https://jgoetzmann.github.io/rk-findings/methodology.html">live on rk-findings</a>.
That page states what the numbers mean; this one explains why the machinery around them is
built the way it is, and what was actually exercised before the run was left alone. The system
itself is drawn on the <a href="architecture.html">architecture page</a>; the paper trail of
choices is on <a href="design-decisions.html">design decisions</a>.</p>

<h2>The shape of the problem</h2>
<p>An unattended search, partly steered by a language model, produces numbers for months with
nobody watching. Numbers from such a system are worth exactly as much as the guarantees around
them: that the scoring rules could not drift, that the searching process could not touch its
own scorer, and that a crash or a stray operator cannot silently corrupt the record. So the
validation story is really three gates, engaged in order: once at container start, once
inside every cycle, and continuously from the host.</p>

<h2>The gate at container start</h2>
<p>The container's entrypoint refuses to run science before proving its environment, in a fixed
order. It writes a heartbeat immediately — the gate takes long enough that the host watchdog
once killed a fresh container fourteen seconds after start, because the heartbeat file on disk
predated the restart; the fix is the very first line of the entrypoint, not a longer timeout.
It then probes its own mount: if <code>/harness</code> accepts a write, the container exits
rather than run with a writable scorer. It recomputes the verifier hash and compares it to the
pinned value. Finally it runs the golden and canary tests — G1 through G20 plus the K1 and K2
canaries, 55 cases at the pre-flight run — with pytest, against the harness as mounted. Any
failure is exit 1 and the runner never starts.</p>
<p>The point of running tests at start rather than at build time: "the tests passed when the
image was built" claims nothing about the harness that is mounted today. The gate re-earns the
claim on every start, including every automatic restart after a kill.</p>

<h2>Ten files, one hash</h2>
<p>Six modules decide every score: coefficient representation, the order conditions, the
verifier, the cost model, the evaluator, and the problem set. Four fixture files pin their
golden values. A sha256 over those ten files, concatenated in a fixed order, is pinned in
<code>VERIFIER_HASH</code>; the entrypoint recomputes and compares it, and every archive record
stores the hash that scored it. A change to any byte of the ten is therefore loud twice over:
the container refuses to start, and a record produced under a different hash would advertise
the fact in its own row.</p>
<p>This was drilled, not assumed. Pre-flight item A3 altered one line of
<code>coeffrep.py</code> and started the container: exit 1, "VERIFIER HASH MISMATCH — refusing
to run". Restored, the same container gated green and started, the 55 golden and canary cases
passing in under four seconds.</p>

<h2>What the container cannot reach</h2>
<p>The harness mount is read-only (<code>:ro</code> on the docker command line, re-checked by
the entrypoint's write probe). The GitHub credential never enters the container at all: the
env file the container receives is filtered on the host and drops every
<code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> line, the runner only ever commits into the mounted work and findings
checkouts, and the host watchdog pushes those commits with the owner's own credentials every
ten minutes. The Codex OAuth file is mounted read-only for the plan-billed planning calls.</p>
<p>The original design scoped a token instead: push rights on rk-work and rk-findings, with a
403 on rk-harness as an acceptance test. That plan failed honestly. The pre-flight scope probe
showed the owner's actual token could push the verifier repo, and the earlier 403 had passed
for an unrelated reason. Moving the credential out of the container entirely makes the
guarantee independent of anyone's permission model — there is no token whose scope can rot.
The full story is under <a href="design-decisions.html#credentials">credentials outside the
container</a>.</p>

<h2>The test suites, tiered</h2>
<p>The suite is organised as numbered tiers that mirror the dependency stack, cheapest and
most foundational at the bottom: T1 covers fixed point, coefficient representation and cycle
counting; T2 the order conditions, evaluator and verifier; T3 the archive, search and
directive validation; T4 the ledger, runner and site generator; T5 the operational config and
the watch view; T6 the Central-time display formatting. Together they collect a bit under a
thousand parametrised cases at this snapshot, with a <code>slow</code> marker separating the
second-scale ones.</p>
<p>Two kinds of test carry most of the weight. Golden tests (G-numbered) pin behaviour to
fixture values written down before the build: the measured order of rk4 comes out 4.0706 from
3 fit points, the hand-counted ARMv6-M multiply sequence costs 13 cycles fast and 75 slow, and
the eleven coefficient representations in the fixture reproduce bit for bit. Canary tests
(K-numbered) are anti-gaming: K1 plants a candidate tuned on the search set and asserts it can
earn search_only but never heldout_verified; K2 asserts that a winner in a single problem
family that is worse on both aggregates stays unreplicated. The gate at container start is a
selection from this same suite, so the anti-gaming properties are re-proved on the mounted
code every time the container comes up.</p>

<h2>The pre-flight, executed as a program</h2>
<p>The pre-flight checklist (docs/REVIEW.md in the harness repo) is not a document anyone
initials; <code>scripts/preflight.py</code> executes every machine-checkable item, prints the
measured value next to each verdict, and writes the report with a sign-off block
(docs/REVIEW-REPORT.md). Statuses are PASS, FAIL, MANUAL (needs the host, hardware, or a
human), SKIP and INFO; the trust boundary (A), ground truth (B), anchor result (C) and
falsification (K) sections gate, and a FAIL there is a nonzero exit. The full run, suite and
docker drills included, was executed on 2026-08-30: every gating section green, with eight
items that need the host, hardware or a human left marked MANUAL, each carrying its
instructions in the report.</p>
<p>The drills are where the exercise paid for itself, because they act instead of asserting:
A1 actually tries to write <code>/harness</code> from inside a running container; A3 actually
tampers with a verifier file and expects the refusal; D5 deliberately feeds the optimizer the
held-out set and checks the search/held-out gap collapses to zero, then returns on revert —
which is what shows the gap metric measures what it claims. Three of these drills changed the
system before the run started; that story is told on the
<a href="architecture.html">architecture page</a>.</p>

<h2>The operational layer</h2>
<p>Once running, the container answers to a host watchdog that polls every ten seconds and
holds every kill switch outside the container. A heartbeat older than two minutes is a
<code>docker kill</code>; a wrongful kill self-heals, because the restart policy brings the
container back and the start gate re-runs. Spend over the monthly cap, or free disk under five
gigabytes, is a stop that stays down. The laptop on battery is a pause, plugged back in an
unpause; another foreground workload holding the CPU above 50% for thirty seconds is a pause,
back under 30% an unpause. Pause is atomic, so the run resumes mid-cycle exactly where it
stopped.</p>
<p>The run also throttles itself. Auto-stop limits (wall-clock minutes or cycle count) end the
run at the next cycle boundary. The language model is asked for a fresh directive every five
cycles and on every escalation, with the last directive reused in between; calls stop once
Codex reports 80% of the weekly plan used; the web literature digest runs every fifty cycles
and the archive interpretation every twenty-five. All of these are operational knobs in a
workspace config.json edited through <code>configure.py</code>. Scientific thresholds are
deliberately not in that file: changing them would invalidate the archive, which is why they
live in the hash-pinned code instead.</p>
<p>One bookkeeping rule ties the layer together: everything stored — archive records, events,
heartbeat, run state — is UTC, and only human-facing display converts to US Central, through
one small module. The watchdog's staleness arithmetic and the archive's determinism both
depend on storage never carrying a local time.</p>

<h2>What this page does not cover</h2>
<p>The scientific method of the search itself — the nine verifier checks in order, exact
rational order conditions, equal-cycle-budget evaluation, tier assignment, the encourager
ladder — is on the <a href="architecture.html">architecture page</a>, and the current
constants and thresholds are on the findings methodology page
(<a href="findings/methodology.html">snapshot</a>,
<a href="https://jgoetzmann.github.io/rk-findings/methodology.html">live</a>). The findings
site is the machine truth; if this page and that one ever disagree, trust that one.</p>
"""

